From 0e56f14b0ca03afee3cad58abd7c989077c7a3ae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: blue898 <201240079+blue898@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 20:49:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 140/146] HACK: usb: host: ehci-exynos: reset-resume the HSIC
 modem after every suspend

exynos_ehci_suspend() switches the PHYs off and tells the CP so. If the
suspend is then aborted, or woken before the SoC has lost its power, the
controller still has CF set and ehci_resume() signals a plain resume on
the port. The CP, already detached and waiting for the attach handshake,
never answers:

  usb 1-2: port 2 resume error -110

followed by a disconnect and a silent CP reset (20 to 30 s without
network). It happened when the CP woke the phone for a signal strength
report and Android suspended again half a second later.

Take the power-loss path every time the modem is there, as Samsung's
s5p-ehci did, so the CP always comes back by reset-resume.

This does not cover everything: after an aborted suspend the link still
disconnects and enumerates again (1 to 2 s), and a series of aborted
suspends can still end in a CP reset.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/usb/host/ehci-exynos.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/host/ehci-exynos.c b/drivers/usb/host/ehci-exynos.c
index ca6882fe4..57bc8acc8 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/host/ehci-exynos.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/ehci-exynos.c
@@ -423,10 +423,21 @@ static int exynos_ehci_resume(struct device *dev)
 	/* DMA burst Enable */
 	writel(EHCI_INSNREG00_ENABLE_DMA_BURST, EHCI_INSNREG00(hcd->regs));
 
-	ehci_resume(hcd, false);
 #ifdef UGLY_HACK
+	/*
+	 * exynos_ehci_suspend() switched the PHYs off and told the CP so. If the
+	 * suspend is then aborted or woken before the SoC loses power, the
+	 * controller still has CF set and a plain resume signals the port, which
+	 * the CP (already detached, waiting for the attach handshake) never
+	 * answers: "port 2 resume error -110", disconnect, silent CP reset. Take
+	 * the power-loss path every time, as Samsung's s5p-ehci did, so the CP is
+	 * always brought back by reset-resume.
+	 */
+	ehci_resume(hcd, exynos_ehci_host_notify != NULL);
 	if (exynos_ehci_host_notify)
 		exynos_ehci_wait_for_cp(hcd);
+#else
+	ehci_resume(hcd, false);
 #endif
 	return 0;
 }
